A||||11||sprnt96||Oil preference...synthetic or petroleum??||||1||
Z||000000||sprnt96||08-07-2001||10:26 PM||||I am getting my motor rebuilt from top to bottom so now is the time to make the decision...synthetic or petroleum based oil???What are your views/opinions??||||reg||1
Z||000001||PBJ||08-07-2001||10:33 PM||||My opinion,  if you change your oil regualarily, ie 5000kms/3months  you will never have engine failure due to oil.<P>I would not use synthetic in mine because I don't believe it is worth the $,  do you always put in premium fuel???  How far would you want to go?$?||||reg||1
Z||000002||theogre||08-07-2001||11:17 PM||||comparing synthetic oil to super unleaded is.... a bit misguided. And No, I'm not starting a flame fest either.<P>It's well established fact that using a higher grade fuel than an engine needs doesn't help performance/mileage and can actually hurt.<P>True synthetics, like Mobil 1, are a fully lab developed product. The oil can be very carefully tailored for the needs of the engine. This is not posible with crude which always contains some undesired elements of the crude base and byproduct, like sulfer compounds, from the refining process.<P>I'm using Mobil 1 and will stay with it. It's probably about due for changing and it still isn't nearly as filthy as any regular cruide base I've ever used.<P>Some people will tell you putting Mobil 1 in an engine with allot of miles will hurt it. Unless the motor already has serious problems developing this is not true. I started using the stuff at over 150,000. If anything I'm using/leaking less than when I was using crude. I haven't added oil since last fill and it's still well above add mark. With crude by now it would usually be just needing a quart.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000003||Ditz88GT||08-08-2001||11:44 AM||||Orge!, Thanks for your insite on this topic.  I had heard that if you have a lot of miles on an engine that only ran crude bassed oil, you may run into problems.  So, with hearing that I was always worried to switch.  If you switched at 150K I plan to switch this weekend.  I've always been a big fan of Mobile 1, now I will feel a lot better about putting it in my Fiero.<P><P>------------------<BR>~Ditz88GT<P>E-mail: cditzler@hotmail.com<BR>AIM: Ditz88GT<BR>http://go.to/fiero||||reg||14
Z||000004||terryk||08-08-2001||01:37 PM||||Wait 50k miles and look in the valve cover of a petroleum-based and synth. You'll understand then. Use synth if you really want the engine to remain clean and last.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Raydar||08-08-2001||02:18 PM||||Ogre. Help!<P>"Rumor Monger" Dept...<BR>I've heard it said that break-in oil should be crude, but after the engine is broken in, switch to synthetic. <BR>This, because synthetic oil doesn't have the ability to cling to metal parts, and can cause "dry" startups, wiping out cams, lifters and such. <BR>I've also heard that synthetics lubricate <I>so well</I> that piston rings don't seat properly.<BR>Sounds like we have two entirely different schools of thought at work here. Is either of them even remotely correct? Or are they both BS?<P>------------------<BR>Raydar - aka Steve<BR><FONT SIZE="1"><BR>Black 88 Formula.<BR>Red 88 Duke coupe. "The Project" <BR>88 Formula parts car. "The Donor"<BR></FONT><p>[This message has been edited by Raydar (edited 08-08-2001).]||||reg||5
Z||000006||JohnF||08-08-2001||02:36 PM||||My Tech 4 filler cap says use 5W-30.  <P>Over 120K miles and no oil leaking or burning tells me that Mr. Goodwrench and I have done well with both frequency and quality in the last 13 years.||||reg||12
Z||000007||88CoupeV6||08-08-2001||05:40 PM||||I only use Mobil 1 15w-50 in summer.  and don't drive the Fiero in winter  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>------------------<BR>Josh<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://people.ne.mediaone.net/fiero64/Fiero642.jpg">   <BR><B>Also 88 Yellow GT</B> :D  <BR><A HREF="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/AlbumList?u=1249362" TARGET=_blank>My pics</A>||||reg||7
Z||000008||theogre||08-08-2001||07:04 PM||||Mobil 1 is now the factory fill for a growing list of very expensive engines. That says allot about filling after a build. Also says what GM and others think about it causing dry start problems or anything else the rumor mill has churned out.<P>Filling after a rebuild I'd use regular oil for one main reason. It's cheaper and I'd only run that first fill 500-1000 miles before I dumped it. The reason is to get rid of any crap that was left in there after the rebuild. <P>I'd also run a magnetic oil plug. It helps reduce loose metal moving to the oil pump.<P>These won't compensate for a sloppy rebuild. It's just to get the stuff that refused to wash out until the oil picked it up.<P>You can dig around in Motor.com Babcox.com and MotorAge.com to find out more about synthetic oil.||||reg||1
Z||000009||Posthumane||08-08-2001||09:50 PM||||Raydar<BR>What you said about synth oils lubricating too well is true. An engine which has a bit of clearance between parts will take much longer to break in with a synthethic. Todays high price engines, however, like merc and bmw are all blue printed, so they don't really need that break in period. that's why they started filling them with synth. After the break in, synthetic oils are much better if you want the engine to last a long time. They don't have the foreign particles left over from the refining process that were mentioned before, and are better formulated chemically for all engine needs (lube, cooling, cusioning, etc.) I just switched to mobil 1 at about 120k kms. (i think)||||reg||1
Z||000010||Raydar||08-08-2001||10:30 PM||||Excellent! Thanks, guys.<BR>I was aware that some manufacturers were recommending Mobil 1. Both of those rumors came from many years ago. <P>------------------<BR>Raydar - aka Steve<BR><FONT SIZE="1"><BR>Black 88 Formula.<BR>Red 88 Duke coupe. "The Project" <BR>88 Formula parts car. "The Donor"<BR></FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000011||Butter||08-09-2001||01:08 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by theogre:<BR><B><P>Filling after a rebuild I'd use regular oil for one main reason. It's cheaper and I'd only run that first fill 500-1000 miles before I dumped it. The reason is to get rid of any crap that was left in there after the rebuild. <P>I'd also run a magnetic oil plug. It helps reduce loose metal moving to the oil pump.<P>These won't compensate for a sloppy rebuild. It's just to get the stuff that refused to wash out until the oil picked it up.<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I definately agree from my past experiances.  I also recomend nondetergent motor oil for the break in proceedure before putting in the synthetic.  This is so a coating of oil will remain on the parts that don't get much oil put to it.  I have built more motorcycle and VW engines that I can remember.  I use the same oil proceedures for any engine I go threw even my lawn mower.  If it hadn't been for the synthetic in my mower after loaning it to my brother I fugure I would have to do premature engine work on it.  I told him it used a little oil but he never checked it and mowed where places should have been bush hogged first.   He left it about half empty and never even give it a rest.  Yes Synthetic oil works for me. <P>||||reg||1
